==English==

===Etymology===
From {{uder|en|la|[[ipsō]] [[factō]]||by the same fact}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|RP}} {{IPA|en|/ˌɪpsəʊ ˈfæktəʊ/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Persent101-ipso facto.wav|Audio (US)}}

===Adverb===
{{en-adv|-|head=ipso facto}}

# By that very fact itself; [[actually]]. Compare: [[eo ipso]].
#* {{RQ:Fuller Bertram Cope|page=94|passage=Cope was not long in feeling him as operating on the unconscious assumption—unconscious, and therefore all the more damnable—that the young man in business constituted, '''ipso facto''', a kind of norm by which other young men in other fields of endeavor were to be gauged: {{...}}}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1999|month=April|author=w:Bryan Caplan|titleurl=http://www.jstor.org/stable/1061278|title=The Austrian Search for Realistic Foundations|journal=Southern Economic Journal|volume=65|number=4|page=833
|passage=For [[w:Ludwig von Mises|[Ludwig von] Mises]] or [[w:Murray Rothbard|[Murray] Rothbard]], it is simply confused to posit latent preferences; if two individuals fail to make an exchange, then this '''ipso facto''' demonstrates that at that moment at least one of them would not have benefited from the exchange.}}

====Related terms====
* [[per se]]

